<section>
<title>Problems</title>
<exercise xml:id="problem_ho-undamped-num">
<statement>
<p>
For a certain mass-spring system the acceleration is given by <m>a_x(t) =
  -0.10\, x(t)</m>. Suppose the initial position and velocity are <m>x(0)
  = 10</m> m and <m>v_x(0) = -1.0\, \mbox{m/s}</m>. Calculate <m>x(t)</m> and <m>v_x(t)</m>
at <m>t = 2\, \mbox{s}</m> in two different ways:
<ol marker="a)">
<li><p>Use two steps of 1 second each.</p></li>
<li><p>Use four steps of <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> second each.  Round only your
    final results to three digits (keep all digits for the
    intermediate calculations).</p></li>
<li><p>Why aren't the answers to a) and b) the same?</p></li>
</ol>

</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
A drag force on an object is opposite to its velocity and is often
proportional to its speed. Let's immerse the mass-spring system of
problem <xref ref="problem_ho-undamped-num" /> in a vat of salad oil so
that the acceleration becomes
<me>
  a_x(t) = -0.10\, x(t) - v_x(t)
</me>
</p>

<p>
Repeat <xref ref="problem_ho-undamped-num"></xref>
for this acceleration. Compare the results with those you
originally got in <xref ref="problem_ho-undamped-num"></xref>. Are the
results what you might expect when a drag force is present?
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</section>
